{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '176,295p'; rg --files collab/yang-clojure | rg '1794519755000|reverification'\"","aggregated_output":"   176\t## 4. R1 durable correspondence schema D1\n   177\t\n   178\t### 4.1 Additive envelope\n   179\t\n   180\tDefine a new separately addressed plain-data object. The following field names and types specify D1; angle-bracket placeholders are metavariables, not values to persist.\n   181\t\n   182\t```clojure\n   183\t{:yang.correspondence/schema 1\n   184\t :yang.correspondence/evidence <Jing address of OracleEvidence>\n   185\t :yang.correspondence/recipe <Jing address of reconstruction recipe>\n   186\t :yang.correspondence/inputs [<Jing address> ...]\n   187\t :yang.correspondence/roots [<canonical UAST root address> ...]\n   188\t :yang.correspondence/origin-bindings\n   189\t [[<ephemeral site id from OracleEvidence> <persisted occurrence key>] ...]\n   190\t :yang.correspondence/artifacts\n   191\t [{:format <existing format keyword>\n   192\t   :identity <that format's identity>\n   193\t   :storage <Jing address of retrievable artifact>\n   194\t   :derivation <Jing address of applicable derivation record>} ...]\n   195\t :yang.correspondence/metadata <Jing address of C1 frontend-metadata rows>\n   196\t :yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance\n   197\t [<Jing address of an instruction-provenance relation> ...]}\n   198\t```\n   199\t\n   200\tThe D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n   201\t\n   202\t`evidence` resolves to **OracleEvidence verbatim**: same EDN data shape, schema/version, field names, vectors, site ids, source-hash value and classifications. Do not project a subset or copy its rows into a second durable site-table shape. Store the whole value through a lossless codec and prove round-trip equality; preserve the original checked-in EDN bytes separately as a source artifact when byte-level archival is needed. Jing storage addressing does not replace the oracle's own canonical-source-hash algorithm. New adoption evidence must use the oracle format as frozen; unsupported representational needs require an explicit versioned extension owned by that format, never an ad hoc competing table.\n   203\t\n   204\t`inputs` names the retrievable source/adaptation/fixture/profile inputs required by the recipe, including the inlined adapter and declarations that the oracle actually hashed. A repository commit string alone is not a fetchable reconstruction input. `roots` names the produced code trees. Expansion records and admitted source-envelope identities required by the bindings must be in the retrievable input closure. The input/source artifacts may be data blobs; they must not be represented as UAST addresses unless they actually are UAST.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe recipe is a closed versioned data description, proposed as:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:yang.correspondence.recipe/schema 1\n   210\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/function <qualified interpreter identifier>\n   211\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/profile <address of exact reconstruction profile>\n   212\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/input-roles [[<role keyword> <input index>] ...]\n   213\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/evidence-contract <oracle's exact schema identity>\n   214\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/coverage <address of declared coverage policy>}\n   215\t```\n   216\t\n   217\tThe recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n   218\t\n   219\t`origin-bindings` is an added relation binding evidence-local sites to actual durable occurrences; **it is not a replacement attribution table**. It preserves the oracle's ephemeral ids literally. Validate every endpoint, reconstructed tree and path. A site's mapping must be unique within the claimed compilation. Multiple replay records can name the same durable occurrence only where they explicitly replay the same admission; distinct admissions must remain distinct. An oracle run without durable expansion/source records cannot be promoted merely by changing `:ephemeral` to `:expansion`. It may remain useful oracle evidence without a durable binding. To accept a relying consumer, reproduce the actual compilation under recorded durable admissions/expansions and verify its correspondence; do not retroactively assert an unrecorded event.\n   220\t\n   221\t### 4.2 Independent reconstruction and discovery\n   222\t\n   223\tThe D1 producer is the compilation/publication composition that can retain inputs, origins and outputs. The canonical compiler and lowerer remain oblivious to attribution policy except for the side artifacts they emit. D1 is an ordinary record on a separate publication/ledger stream, discoverable by its address and its code/evidence associations. The Python site-set record is a concrete precedent for “record names manifest, manifest keeps schema” (`siteset.cljc:5-22,79-101`). The implementation must pin the actual discovery schema, signature/index projection and retention owner before acceptance; this document does not pretend the current linker automatically fetches D1.\n   224\t\n   225\tVerification proceeds in this order:\n   226\t\n   227\t1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n   228\t2. Verify each code image under its own execution contract and its existing derivation chain. Report content integrity separately from code derivation.\n   229\t3. Reconstruct the source/adaptation compilation under the exact recipe, using recorded source admissions and expansion attempts; recompute final roots and eligible occurrence sets. Recompute the oracle's canonical-source-hash using its exact rule.\n   230\t4. Independently re-derive the oracle site declarations from compiled AST argument expressions. Compare with OracleEvidence verbatim under that schema's comparison rules. Do not make the producer's serialized classifications the verifier's input oracle.\n   231\t5. Reconstruct every claimed ephemeral-to-persisted binding, C1 marker set and instruction mapping. Verify exact coverage and all multiplicities, including helpers that receive no upstream credit.\n   232\t6. Execute the attribution query over separately scoped evidence/occurrence/row relations; require actual body entry in both required oracle C lanes. Reconstruct the credit set and report withdrawn witnesses and stale declarations. R7 requires re-derivation, not just re-running a query over trusted declarations (`ruling-of-record.md:48-50`).\n   233\t7. Publish a separate verification result naming D1's address, recipe/profile, verifier revision, inputs, outcomes and evidence. D1 cannot mark itself “verified”. The first additional consumer's acceptance must cite this completed result.\n   234\t\n   235\tAn implementation may share the schema decoder and canonical primitives, but a verifier must not call the producer's table derivation as its only “independent” check. The existing table generator resolves the oracle's own private derivation vars (`latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-18`); regeneration through it alone does not satisfy R7.\n   236\t\n   237\tD1 does not revise old site tables, force old module manifests to schema 2, or automatically validate the oracle's historical evidence. If actual integration requires changing a closed publication record, that specific schema revision returns to the A2 inventory. Unknown recipes refuse verification; `:trusted` is never substituted for `:verifying` (linker design `:1902-1921`).\n   238\t\n   239\t## 5. A3 adoption briefs and ordering\n   240\t\n   241\t**Scheduling:** frontend adoption starts after the in-flight cell-ref sweeps and the J2 re-run land. Refresh this inventory on that merged base before changing shared prelude/publication paths. The current sweeps, J2 and oracle continue under their existing scopes. This design does not merge, pause, or extend their tasks (signed A3/A4 at lines 21–25).\n   242\t\n   243\tEach adoption brief must cite this findings file and C1/D1 by revision. The shared occurrence finalizer/validator and provenance publication work precede or accompany frontend implementation; D1 independent verification precedes acceptance of the first frontend consumer that relies on D1. A frontend may adopt static annotation without consuming oracle attribution, but cannot call that durable correspondence acceptance.\n   244\t\n   245\t### 5.1 Mandatory host sets\n   246\t\n   247\tThese are acceptance requirements, not claims that current tests already pass. **No frontend may drop Jolt because shared code compiled on another host.** Host lanes and VM execution formats are separate axes.\n   248\t\n   249\t| Frontend | JVM | Node | Dart | Jolt |\n   250\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   251\t| Production Clojure | Reader/source, all production entry points, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same portable source/forms and paths, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same | Same; run current reader limitations as explicit refusals, not substitute parsers |\n   252\t| TypeScript / JS dialect | Local source parser plus portable CST lowering, bundled/linked/prelude publications and load/query | Same pinned source-derived CST through portable lowering; parserless import/load; fresh prelude publication | Same packet/import/publication gate | Same packet/import/publication gate; Jolt load/parity is required |\n   253\t| PHP ANTLR + legacy PHP | Local ANTLR source parser, portable packet paths, includes/class-map/SCC, both compiler families and publications | Same source-derived CST for ANTLR; legacy source compiler; fresh publications and imports | Same | Same packet/legacy source/publication/load gate |\n   254\t| Python | Local source parser, packet/module/import/SCC/session paths, publications | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Native C++ Python parser plus packet/session/publication/load paths; unavailable parser is a failed required source gate with a recorded reason, not a green skip |\n   255\t\n   256\tTS local parser restriction is recorded at `docs/design/yang.typescript.md:113-116,517-518`; its packet-level execution policy at `:1325-1326`. Python's host selection is implemented in `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:71-110`; Jolt's native Python adapter describes its actual parser and refusal in `src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:1-28`. PHP's local ANTLR parser is `src/clj/yang/php/antlr/cst.clj`, while the independent portable legacy compiler remains registered at `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:69-70`. Existing Jolt applicability exclusions are explicit per-test data (`test/resources/yin/repl/jolt-applicability.edn:1-5,46-48`); they cannot be expanded to evade these new gates.\n   257\t\n   258\tOn each host run static occurrence/provenance checks for AST, semantic, stack and register artifacts where the existing format admits the fixture. Execute direct walker and all three vector paths for the portable correctness corpus. Linked paths exercise all supported linked formats and pin existing unsupported/refusal cases explicitly. For example, the inspected TS linked harness documents a walker `:undeclared-free` limitation while using three vector VMs (`test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11-15`); this design does not silently promise that old unrelated limitation is fixed. If a missing lane prevents the required new provenance test, report adoption incomplete with the named dependency.\n   259\t\n   260\t### 5.2 Cross-host equality without collapsing admissions\n   261\t\n   262\tBuild a portable replay fixture containing the SAME source or source-derived CST, grammar/options, compiler/profile revision, canonical naming inputs, source-medium/batch/member coordinates, and expansion environment/attempt inputs. The source origin contract explicitly makes these coordinates supplied plain data (`code-as-tuples.md:405-437`; `vm.cljc:938-958`).\n   263\t\n   264\tRequire:\n   265\t\n   266\t- Identical final canonical roots and the full set of `[origin root path]` keys on JVM, Node and Dart; include Jolt in the same comparison for the mandatory portable paths.\n   267\t- Identical C1 rows, reconstructed site declarations, and per-format instruction provenance. Different formats have different PCs, but they must resolve to the same intended application K.\n   268\t- For expansion fixtures, identical expansion-record addresses under replayed identities and identical producing-record chains. Normalize neither origin nor generated names away after the fact.\n   269\t- For TS/PHP packet routes, compare compilation of the same source-derived validated CST; record the JVM parser artifact. This proves compilation equality, not nonexistent local-parser parity. Python additionally compares source parsing/export across its real local parsers, following the existing exporter conformance model (`test/yang/antlr/conformance.cljc:1-20`).\n   270\t- A separate negative fixture admits identical text twice with distinct supplied admission tokens and demands different origins. Cross-host equality is replay equality; it must not turn occurrences into content identities.\n   271\t\n   272\tHost-specific source/reader conditionals that intentionally yield different programs must have separately declared cohorts and expected refusals. They cannot explain divergence for the identical portable input cohort. Include floats, metadata, generated temporaries and duplicate trees in the portable cohort because root equality is part of the gate, not just path equality.\n   273\t\n   274\t### 5.3 Frontend-specific work packages\n   275\t\n   276\t**Clojure.** Inventory native/argument-vector/outcome compile options, `compile` vs `compile-program`, `compile-form` callers, macro expansion, `lower-unit`, `library-for`, direct walker/datoms/rows lanes, REPL single/multi-form and linked runtime publication. Show a non-oracle production fixture traversing the full pipeline, including macro-generated and helper calls. Republish `clj` and `yang.call` on each host. Oracle-specific stamping is instantiation #1 only; it does not close this brief. Anchors: `yang/clojure.cljc:2021-2034,2164-2200,2791,2839-2862,2993-3021`.\n   277\t\n   278\t**TypeScript.** Cover `lower-module-body`, `lower-unit`, `lower-units`, `lower-packet`, streaming stage, bundled and linked `js`, `yang.call`, and safepoint runtime. Track new publication/SCC/session paths from the seats when they land; do not infer their implementation from design prose. Generated member/constructor/runtime dispatch applications get their own K; source-call attribution is a separate query. Anchors: `lower.cljc:1167-1211,2262-2363`; `prelude.cljc:4168-4260`.\n   279\t\n   280\t**PHP.** Cover ANTLR and still-registered legacy compiler, include/classmap/import publication, SCC bundles/aliases, strict-types variants, prelude definition lists and P9 additions. Test by-reference/indirect calls, generators and exceptional helper paths without borrowing source-call credit. Republish `php`, dependencies and all include/import fixtures. Anchors: `php.cljc:640-685`; ANTLR `lower.cljc:1710,1812,2082,2207,3737-4041`; `publication.cljc:27-70`; `prelude_module.cljc:386`; `prelude_p9.cljc:558`.\n   281\t\n   282\t**Python.** Cover packet and module lowering, import units, SCC aliases, bundled/linked `py`, `yang.call`, `pysp`, hook staging/site-set discovery, and both fresh/primed REPL sessions. Do not conflate `:yang/site` hook sites with complete application occurrence coverage. Republish the DHT fixture and validate parserless imports on other hosts. Anchors: `lower.cljc:1111-1120,1775,2018-2373`; `repl.cljc:86-108`; `publication.cljc:51-80`; `siteset.cljc:5-31`.\n   283\t\n   284\tEach brief's acceptance bundle contains: exact producer/artifact checklist; input/commit/profile pins; C1 coverage counts and root/hash invariance; four-host results and cross-host equality report; correctness battery results; before/after budgets and measurements; legacy-load/missing-provenance refusal; and D1 verification result if it relies on correspondence. No frontend can satisfy this bundle merely by running the oracle test.\n   285\t\n   286\t## 6. Measurement and declared budgets\n   287\t\n   288\tThese are measurement requirements and design cost models, **not measured performance claims**. Signed A3 requires a budget impact per frontend, and line 29 requires static/runtime separation. This read-only design has no authority to invent benchmark results. Numeric per-corpus totals remain **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides by measuring the pinned baseline before tagged runs**.\n   289\t\n   290\t### 6.1 Counts and cost model\n   291\t\n   292\tFor each fixture/prelude/module, host, and format record:\n   293\t\n   294\t- `U`: distinct canonical rows; `O`: all occurrence-tree positions; `A`: `:application` occurrences; `D`: maximum path depth; `L`: total encoded length of application paths; separate user, helper, wrapper, prelude, macro-generated and Rule-R counts as derived diagnostic groups.\n   295\t- `P_f`: instruction count/provenance-row count for format f; report shared semantic/native mapping structures separately if used.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
