{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '1,40p;535,580p;780,840p'; rg -n 'defn.*map|read-map|decode-map|assoc' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | tail -25; rg -n 'cc3-oracle-binding|kernel-canonical-source-hash|kernel-trace-form' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.jing.cbor\n     2\t  \"DaoJing's canonical CBOR value codec and portable numeric operations\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, phases J1 and J2; portable writer in slice H4).\n     4\t\n     5\t   `encode` turns one supported value into its canonical payload bytes;\n     6\t   `decode` accepts exactly one canonical payload and returns its value.\n     7\t   The byte contract is the frozen corpus test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\n     8\t   and the rulings in its README; this namespace implements it, it does not\n     9\t   define it.\n    10\t\n    11\t   Encoding normalizes a value and directly emits canonical CBOR bytes over\n    12\t   `dao.host.*`:\n    13\t\n    14\t   * keywords and symbols: tag 27 `dao.jing/keyword` / `dao.jing/symbol`\n    15\t     over `[namespace name]`, read from the identifier's fields;\n    16\t   * lists and finite seqs: tag 27 `dao.jing/list` over the item array;\n    17\t     vectors are plain arrays;\n    18\t   * floating point: tag 27 `dao.jing/float64` over eight big-endian bytes,\n    19\t     float32 widened exactly, every NaN made 7ff8000000000000;\n    20\t   * integers: CBOR integers, tags 2/3 beyond 64 bits;\n    21\t   * decimals: tag 4 `[exponent mantissa]`; ratios: tag 30, reduced;\n    22\t   * sets: tag 258 over elements sorted by their canonical bytes;\n    23\t   * metadata on collections and symbols: tag 27 `clojure/with-meta` over\n    24\t     `[meta value]`, outermost, after stripping unqualified :line :column\n    25\t     :end-line :end-column (validated before stripping) and omitting empty\n    26\t     metadata.\n    27\t\n    28\t   Decoding reads the bytes with Jing's own structural reader, which checks\n    29\t   every tag, frame name and payload shape before any host conversion (the\n    30\t   plan's \\\"validate before any lossy host conversion\\\"), then requires the\n    31\t   decoded value to re-encode to the identical bytes.\n    32\t\n    33\t   Every refusal is an ex-info whose data carries `::refusal`, one of the\n    34\t   corpus refusal classes as a keyword (`:non-canonical`,\n    35\t   `:unpaired-surrogate`, ...); `refusal` reads it.\n    36\t\n    37\t   Numbers: the JVM keeps its native exact kinds (long, BigInt, Double,\n    38\t   BigDecimal, Ratio) and adds a `Rational` carrier for denominator-1\n    39\t   ratios. ClojureScript uses safe-integer numbers and BigInt for integers,\n    40\t   and carriers for float64, decimal and rational content. Host `=` cannot\n   535\t              (str (big (decimal-mantissa x)))]\n   536\t    :rational (let [[_ n d] (exact x)]\n   537\t                [::rational (str n) (str d)])))\n   538\t\n   539\t\n   540\t(defn content-key\n   541\t  \"A host value whose host `=` and `hash` agree with content=: numbers by\n   542\t   their kind-strict identity, byte strings by content, identifiers by their\n   543\t   namespace and name fields, lists and vectors alike (README ruling A6)\n   544\t   element by element, maps and sets by members, metadata ignored, any other\n   545\t   value as itself. A caller keys a host map or set by it to get\n   546\t   kind-strict content identity from host collections (JVM `=` merges\n   547\t   0.0 with -0.0 and 1.0M with 1.00M; ClojureScript merges slash-crossed\n   548\t   identifiers). The same traversal as equiv, with the kind-strict numeric\n   549\t   key in place of num=.\"\n   550\t  [x]\n   551\t  (cond\n   552\t    (numeric? x) (numeric-content-key x)\n   553\t    (byte-payload? x) [::bytes (bytes-hex x)]\n   554\t    (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) [::identifier (identifier-key x)]\n   555\t    (sequential-value? x) (mapv content-key x)\n   556\t    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(content-key k) (content-key v)])) x)\n   557\t    (set? x) (into #{} (map content-key) x)\n   558\t    :else x))\n   559\t\n   560\t\n   561\t(defn content=\n   562\t  \"Portable kind-strict decoded equality: two values are content= exactly\n   563\t   when content addressing would give them the same identity. Numbers must\n   564\t   share kind (integer, float64, decimal, rational), and within kind the\n   565\t   float zero sign, the decimal scale and the exact value; host integer\n   566\t   width is not significant, and canonical NaNs are equal (every NaN has\n   567\t   the one canonical encoding). Collections recurse the way equiv does,\n   568\t   metadata ignored. Unlike equiv, (content= 1 1.0) is false.\"\n   569\t  [a b]\n   570\t  (or (identical? a b)\n   571\t      (= (content-key a) (content-key b))))\n   572\t\n   573\t\n   574\t(defn content-hash\n   575\t  \"A hash consistent with content=. Host-specific like num-hash: never\n   576\t   compare hash values across hosts.\"\n   577\t  [x]\n   578\t  (hash (content-key x)))\n   579\t\n   580\t\n   780\t   Returns [[key-bytes key-bytes-hex value-bytes-or-nil] ...].\"\n   781\t  [pairs set? dup-class d]\n   782\t  (let [wired (mapv (fn [[k v]]\n   783\t                      (let [kb (value-bytes k d)\n   784\t                            vb (when-not set? (value-bytes v d))]\n   785\t                        [kb (bytes-hex kb) vb]))\n   786\t                    pairs)]\n   787\t    (when (< (count (set (map second wired))) (count wired))\n   788\t      (refuse dup-class))\n   789\t    (when (collapse? (map first pairs))\n   790\t      (refuse :equality-collapse))\n   791\t    wired))\n   792\t\n   793\t\n   794\t(defn- map-bytes\n   795\t  \"A map at depth d; keys and values one level deeper.\"\n   796\t  [m d]\n   797\t  (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   798\t  (let [wired (members-bytes (seq m) false :duplicate-key (inc d))\n   799\t        sorted (sort #(compare-canonical-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) wired)\n   800\t        pair-buffers (mapcat (fn [[kb _ vb]] [kb vb]) sorted)]\n   801\t    (concat-buffers (cons (write-head 5 (count wired)) pair-buffers))))\n   802\t\n   803\t\n   804\t(defn- set-bytes\n   805\t  \"Tag 258 at depth d over an array at d+1; elements at d+2.\"\n   806\t  [s d]\n   807\t  (deep! (inc d) :unsupported-value)\n   808\t  (let [wired (members-bytes (map (fn [x] [x nil]) s)\n   809\t                             true\n   810\t                             :duplicate-element\n   811\t                             (+ d 2))\n   812\t        sorted (sort #(compare-canonical-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) wired)\n   813\t        item-buffers (map first sorted)\n   814\t        arr (concat-buffers (cons (write-head 4 (count wired)) item-buffers))]\n   815\t    (concat-buffers [(write-head 6 258) arr])))\n   816\t\n   817\t\n   818\t(defn- meta-bytes\n   819\t  \"The bytes of x at depth d, where (build depth) encodes x's own\n   820\t   content starting at that depth. If x carries metadata that survives\n   821\t   stripping, a with-meta frame sits at d and x's content moves two\n   822\t   levels deeper (tag, [meta value]). Stripped values are validated\n   823\t   first (ruling A9); a metadata map carrying its own metadata is\n   824\t   refused (ruling A3).\"\n   825\t  [x d build]\n   826\t  (let [m (meta x)]\n   827\t    (if (nil? m)\n   828\t      (build d)\n   829\t      (do (when (some? (meta m))\n   830\t            (refuse :malformed-frame \"metadata map carries metadata\"))\n   831\t          (doseq [k reader-position-keys\n   832\t                  :when (contains? m k)]\n   833\t            (value-bytes (get m k) (+ d 3)))\n   834\t          (let [kept (apply dissoc m reader-position-keys)]\n   835\t            (if (empty? kept)\n   836\t              (build d)\n   837\t              (let [name-bytes (host text/utf8-enco