{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '165,204p'; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '65,100p'; rg -n 'print-namespace-maps|print-dup|print-readably' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj deps.edn; nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794519755000-engineer-post-port-oracle-reverification.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '38,108p'\"","aggregated_output":"   165\tother tagged literals are addressable today and have no slot in this\n   166\tsupported-values list or in Jing's four named CBOR extensions; they become\n   167\trejected, unsupported values once this plan lands. No current producer\n   168\temits them, so nothing observed breaks, but \"existing stores... must be\n   169\trebuilt together\" (*Addressing and clean break*) presumes every stored\n   170\tvalue is re-encodable under the new domain — an assumption this delta\n   171\tshould be checked against before rebuild, not discovered during it.\n   172\t\n   173\t- Normalize sorted collections to ordinary maps and sets. Sort map keys\n   174\t  and set elements lexicographically by unsigned canonical encoded bytes\n   175\t  (a proper prefix sorts first), not length-first. Reject duplicate canonical\n   176\t  keys or elements introduced by normalization instead of losing entries.\n   177\t  Supported maps/sets must also survive portable decoded equality without\n   178\t  collapsing entries: reject such collisions on encode and before decoded\n   179\t  collection construction, including numerically equal keys of different\n   180\t  kinds. Do not let the accepting host determine this boundary.\n   181\t- Encode strings as UTF-8 without Unicode normalization. Reject unpaired\n   182\t  high or low surrogates before encoding on every host, including in\n   183\t  identifier components and metadata; normal astral text and noncharacters\n   184\t  remain legal. Reject invalid UTF-8 on decode. Boring's own surrogate\n   185\t  pre-validation is unverified; Jing owns this rule regardless.\n   186\t- Lists and sequences share a representation distinct from vectors. Use\n   187\t  CBOR tag 27 with the name `dao.jing/list` and an array argument; plain\n   188\t  CBOR arrays represent vectors.\n   189\t- Preserve collection and symbol metadata using Boring's `clojure/with-meta`\n   190\t  mapping. Strip `:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, and `:end-column`; omit empty\n   191\t  metadata. Clear metadata on internally constructed Dart collections\n   192\t  before attaching supplied metadata. Do not strip arbitrary user `:tag`\n   193\t  metadata as a workaround for Dart constructor metadata. This clearing\n   194\t  covers only collections Jing constructs at decode: a Dart producer\n   195\t  building a payload with `list` must still clear ClojureDart's constructor\n   196\t  metadata itself (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`), and so must internal\n   197\t  producers reconstructing lists in Jing-bound code, such as the de Bruijn\n   198\t  projection; that `dao.jing.md` open item carries forward, it is not\n   199\t  retired by this plan.\n   200\t- Encode **all** keywords and symbols as tag 27 with `dao.jing/keyword` or\n   201\t  `dao.jing/symbol` and `[namespace name]`, where namespace is nil or a\n   202\t  string and name is a string, taken directly from the identifier's fields.\n   203\t  Uniform escaping removes a host-sensitive ordinary/escaped predicate.\n   204\t  Never parse or print these components to reconstruct an identifier;\n    65\tFinalize paths only after macro expansion, generated-name canonicalization, wrappers, module/SCC assembly, and tail marking that determine the named tree. Source-level annotations alone cannot cover generated calls (base ruling R2 at line 17). Low-level functions returning fragments may defer finalization until an owning root and origin exist; each executable/publication ingress must invoke the common finalizer. A frontend cannot claim adoption by marking just its parser or user-call lowering arm.\n    66\t\n    67\tA subsequent tree-changing transform creates another root and another occurrence relation. It must preserve an explicit old/new occurrence correspondence where it makes an attribution claim, recompute C1 for new applications, and identify generated helper calls separately. It must not retain stale paths or equate every new helper with the nearest source call. If a transform deletes, duplicates, or merges occurrences, its correspondence must record the actual multiplicity; ambiguity refuses attribution.\n    68\t\n    69\tMacro expansion already records input root, call path, macro root, parent, output and attempt (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1000,1062-1119`). However, final tail marking and stand-in lowering happen later (`:1255-1260`). The implementation inventory must resolve how final published roots are related to these expansion outputs. Whether the existing event/record adapters already provide the complete final-root correspondence is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. Do not assume an intermediate output record identifies every occurrence in the final assembled tree, and do not mint a fake `:expand` merely to satisfy the shape.\n    70\t\n    71\t### 2.4 Lowering, reconstruction, and runtime lookup\n    72\t\n    73\tPersist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n    74\t\n    75\tSegment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n    76\t\n    77\tCanonical reconstruction deliberately shares nodes by content (`vm.cljc:1477-1486,1522-1525`). Never cache an occurrence annotation by node-id or mutate a shared canonical reconstruction and expect distinct sites to survive. Keep an occurrence cursor/side lookup, or expand occurrence-specific ephemeral images. The datom linearizer currently returns `[pc entity]`, not K (`semantic/linearize.cljc:283-302`); use an explicit entity-to-occurrence bridge with the owning batch, root and path, refusing ambiguity. `encoder/datom-entity-occurrences` and the envelope's `:yin/entity-occurrences` are the starting machinery (`encoder.cljc:67,115-148`).\n    78\t\n    79\t### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n    80\t\n    81\tName the nonpersistent domain **ephemeral instrumentation origin**. Its C1 spelling is:\n    82\t\n    83\t```clojure\n    84\t[:ephemeral evidence-scope-token compilation-token member-index]\n    85\t;; ephemeral site:\n    86\t[ephemeral-origin canonical-compiled-root structural-path]\n    87\t;; already signed trace shape:\n    88\t[fname arity caller-site-id]\n    89\t```\n    90\t\n    91\tThe composition supplies two non-nil plain-data tokens: `evidence-scope-token` names the recording/fixture scope; `compilation-token` names this particular instrumented compilation in that scope. `member-index` is a nonnegative integer. They are explicitly recorded inputs, not inferred from source hash, host object identity, time, lane name, or callee. Both oracle C lanes execute the SAME compiled artifact with the same tokens; a separate compilation gets another token. Replay uses the recorded tokens. A source hash alone would collapse distinct attempts.\n    92\t\n    93\tThis is a precise proposed spelling for the still-unfrozen oracle implementation. It is **not** a third legal persisted occurrence origin: `vm/occurrence-origin?` must continue rejecting it. An EDN evidence file may preserve an ephemeral id as evidence of that run; that does not convert it into a portable code occurrence. Persisted C1 side tables and expansion links require real ruled origins.\n    94\t\n    95\tThe canonical compiled root is computed after code-producing instrumentation that changes the AST, before occurrence stamps are associated into runtime maps. Runtime stamping must not introduce a root-hash self-reference. If instrumentation subsequently changes structure, it must carry an explicit transform mapping or recompute the frozen site table; no path can silently retain its old meaning.\n    96\t\n    97\tThe oracle must finalize the spelling in its own versioned evidence contract and use that format once. If its accepted schema differs by the time implementation starts, preserve its evidence verbatim and publish a reviewed binding of its origin fields to this semantic domain; do not rewrite its tables behind an unchanged version. A semantic conflict (no compilation scope, map-key paths, callee ids) requires correction on the oracle's record, not a silent normalization in durable verification.\n    98\t\n    99\tRuntime `assoc` stamping is explicitly licensed by §2.6, owner ruling 2026-09-15: the walker holds an ephemeral image and nothing runtime-associated is projected back to canonical rows; runtime keys must be stripped at serialization/projection of continuation frames (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:459-474`). Static frontend marker extraction is a separate, pre-execution side-table operation. This clause licenses oracle-specific walker instrumentation; it does not establish ordinary production coverage.\n   100\t\n    38\t\n    39\tThe binding test green (item 1) covers both concrete checks: the per-lane K sets are equal\n    40\t(`(= (lane-keys :c-walker) (lane-keys :c-semantic))` green), and the expected occurrence\n    41\tmultiset is INDEPENDENTLY RECOMPUTED from the post-port compiled tree\n    42\t(`cc3-walk (:compiled-ast compiled)` minus Rule-R `yin/def` applications) and verified equal to\n    43\tsemantic, stack, and register call provenance — exact call-PC coverage and multiplicities.\n    44\t\n    45\t**The binding WAS re-derived by the port — in Phase 3r, commit `8456c2e9` (\"port Phase 3r —\n    46\tkernel-gate corpus off the outcome model\"), NOT Phase 4.** Phase 4 (`35d09aaa`) touched only\n    47\t`test/yang/clojure_test.clj` (verified by `--stat`). What the rebind changed, exhaustively\n    48\t(diff `8afd82f5..309fe842` on `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, cc3 section):\n    49\t\n    50\t1. **Stamping wrapper shape**: pre-port each stamp inserted THREE applications around the\n    51\t   argument node (`data/nth` outcome-unwrap over `dsg__intra_call` over `conj`); post-port TWO\n    52\t   (`dsg__intra_call` over `conj`) — under the continuation lane `gate-site!` returns the\n    53\t   argument vector as a plain value, so the `data/nth` outcome unwrap is gone. `kernel-stamped-args?`\n    54\t   recognizer updated accordingly.\n    55\t2. **Site-id extraction path** (`cc3-stamp-site-id`): `[:operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`\n    56\t   (was `[:operands 0 :operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`).\n    57\t3. **Correspondence translation** (`cc3-correspond` wrapper clause): drops the `[3 0]` step —\n    58\t   wrapped argument now at `[3 1] [3 2]`.\n    59\t4. **Post-transformation coverage accounting**: `inserted` per wrapper = 2 paths (was 3);\n    60\t   count assertion `(+ (count marked) (* 2 (count sites)))` (was `* 3`).\n    61\t5. The duplicate-site-id forgery mutation path and the gate-site! helper-name check moved with\n    62\t   the new wrapper geometry.\n    63\t\n    64\t**Concrete recorded numbers at the post-port tip** (evidence ns `test/yang/item3_evidence.clj`,\n    65\ttemporary, run via `clojure -M:test -n yang.item3-evidence`; same fixture\n    66\t`{:call (latte-kernel.utils/vectorn? [1 2 3] 2)}`, same machinery as the binding test):\n    67\t\n    68\t- origin (the retained admission record): `[:source :program [pp-item3-verify-<uuid> 0] 0]` —\n    69\t  run scope + ordinal 0 + member 0, as r5 threaded.\n    70\t- **2 oracle sites, 2 binding entries** — the SAME site count cc3 recorded pre-port (2 sites,\n    71\t  cc3 findings `1794357169000`); the fixture and its stamping survived the port 1:1.\n    72\t  Sites: `[1 kernel-context [:literal :variable :variable]]`,\n    73\t  `[2 gate-utils-vectorn? [:computed :literal]]`.\n    74\t- **marked 846 = eligible 846** — the full C1 set still carries exactly its markers\n    75\t  (coverage separation). For the SAME fixture cc3 recorded 987 (then 981 after its audit\n    76\t  fixes) application occurrences pre-port; the post-port compiled tree carries 846. The\n    77\t  ~135-141 fewer applications are the retired outcome machinery's generated applications\n    78\t  (outcome-tuple construction/unwrap sites), NOT lost guest code: the equality\n    79\t  eligible = marked holds at both shapes, and the fixture's 2 oracle sites survived 1:1 —\n    80\t  exactly the \"compiled shape moved, binding still resolves\" case the checklist asks about.\n    81\t- **stamped-eligible 850 = 846 + 2 sites x 2 applications** — the post-port wrapper inserts\n    82\t  exactly two applications per stamp, the re-derived accounting, verified numerically.\n    83\t- **direct-stamps 2, routed-stamps 0; 34 intra-unit applications, 0 routed** — this fixture is\n    84\t  entirely intra-unit direct tail application (the port's preserved tail lane); no bridge frames\n    85\t  are involved, so no site's compiled shape moved between tail and bridged for it.\n    86\t- **both lanes** `:completed` (walker AND semantic), 2 executed calls, 2 distinct sites,\n    87\t  every executed site bound; **per-lane K sets EQUAL** (k-set-size 2, re-asserted green by\n    88\t  the evidence ns's own `is`, 2 assertions 0 failures); K root: `:segment/blake3-42028ee1…`\n    89\t  with per-site pre-stamp paths recorded in the run log (below).\n    90\t\n    91\t**What did NOT change**: the binding contract itself — K = [origin root path] with origin = the\n    92\tretained admission record (`:admission-origin`, never content synthesis), the\n    93\tstamped→pre-stamp correspondence discipline (refusals `:cc3-stamp-correspondence`,\n    94\t`:cc3-admission-record`, `:cc3-duplicate-stamp-site`, `:cc3-ambiguous-stamp-correspondence`),\n    95\tthe bijection assertions (stamped guest sites ↔ selected compiled occurrences), and the\n    96\tindependent-occurrence-multiset recomputation. Both C lanes still resolve every executed site to\n    97\tits bound K, identically. The bijection HOLDS at the post-port shape; the wrapper-layer\n    98\taccounting was re-derived to match the retired outcome channel.\n    99\t\n   100\t**D1 evidence-import guidance: pin the POST-port revision.** Any cc3-era binding evidence that\n   101\tassumed the 3-application wrapper (site-id path `[:operands 0 ...]`, 3 inserted applications per\n   102\tstamp) is stale in exactly that layer — pre-port recorded K paths remain valid only if derived\n   103\tbefore stamping (they are: K paths are pre-stamp paths by construction); the stamped-tree\n   104\tcoverage numbers changed shape. Concretely: pin `ba3cc407` (or any revision from `8456c2e9`\n   105\tonward) for binding evidence import; pre-port evidence (`8afd82f5` and earlier) for wrapper-shape\n   106\tcomparisons only.\n   107\t\n   108\t## Item 4 — Provenance side-artifact integrity: PASS\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-13 16:54:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-13 23:54:24 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nRULING: (A) — Reconstruct from admission-captured text under a pinned printing/reading profile; accept each record only when replay reproduces its retained roots and required correspondence artifacts exactly.\n\nThe defect is in D1’s underspecified reconstruction input. Jing deliberately normalizes map/set representation and sorts their encoded members; it does not retain host iteration order. The compiler explicitly turns that order into ordered operands at [clojure.cljc:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:316). Equal values can therefore produce different compiled roots under this lowering. That is an expected consequence of the current contracts, not evidence of Jing corruption.\n\nUnitarity requires retaining the information needed to recover the admitted syntax and its interpretations. An order-insensitive value snapshot is insufficient when the interpreter observes order. Captured text supplies otherwise missing input information; it is not a derived classification that a query over the retained unordered values could recover.\n\nI reject **(B) for this round**: preserving host order in Jing changes its canonical representation contract; canonicalizing compiler traversal changes ordered code output and potentially evaluation effects. Either requires separate design, ownership and profile migration. I reject **(C) as a mandatory contract**: a second decoded program cannot certify ordering or replace the replay gate, and its value can be derived from the retained text. Admission-time comparison with the original in-memory input remains required under (A).\n\nThe following is the normative amendment to [§4.1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:217).\n\n1. **Reconstruction input and capture boundary.**  \n   The recipe’s authoritative reconstruction input is retained text captured from the actual admitted compilation inputs, before their order-observing consumption and before codec normalization. For this Clojure recipe, this includes the assembled program supplied to `yang/compile-program`, printed with metadata. This text is a serialization of admitted forms; it need not be the original handwritten source.\n\n   Retain every additional input required to reproduce source hashing, assembly, expansion, instrumentation and correspondence. In particular, `forms` and `call` must have replay-preserving representations because the existing oracle hash traverses and prints them. Options and other inputs may remain ordinary Jing values only where the profile establishes that codec normalization cannot change their interpretation. Otherwise they require the same text treatment.\n\n   Text is an ordinary addressed source artifact, not a UAST address. Decoded forms are derived working values. Optional diagnostic snapshots confer no reconstruction authority.\n\n2. **Pinned printing/reading profile.**  \n   The addressed profile must specify the exact executable printer and reader implementations, versions, host/runtime, admitted source domain, character encoding, dynamic bindings, reader options and tag handlers.\n\n   The initial JVM profile shall use UTF-8 text without normalization and pin `clojure.core/pr-str` with metadata enabled, unlimited print length/level, readable printing enabled, duplicate printing disabled and namespace-map shorthand disabled. Any further printer behavior affecting admitted values must be pinned or excluded by the admitted domain.\n\n   Reading shall use the pinned EDN reader, accept exactly one expected top-level value and require EOF afterward. Ambient custom readers, implicit namespace resolution and fallback to a different reader are prohibited. Supported tagged forms must have explicitly pinned handlers; unsupported forms refuse reconstruction.\n\n   **Printing and reading are not asserted to preserve every Clojure value or collection order.** The admitted domain and per-input gates determine support. A host that cannot reproduce the profile refuses; this does not waive existing cross-host adoption requirements.\n\n3. **Admission-time fidelity and replay gate.**  \n   Capture from the actual input, then read the captured text and compare it with that input before declaring it reconstructable. The comparison must preserve syntax distinctions, numeric identities and required metadata. `content=` alone is insufficient: its implementation ignores metadata and treats lists and vectors alike.\n\n   Compile the read-back input under the pinned profile and recorded compilation context. Require exact equality between its final canonical root and the root produced from the actual admitted input. Retain and validate the complete referenced row closure.\n\n   After durable storage, repeat reconstruction using only fetched retained inputs and dependencies. Compare with the retained expected root; do not replace that root with the replay result. Each claimed compilation must pass independently. A successful fixture does not license another fixture.\n\n4. **Exactness beyond the root.**  \n   Root equality is necessary, but does not establish D1 verification. Replay must also reproduce the oracle’s existing canonical-source-hash, independently derived site declarations, actual admission/expansion associations, complete C1 relation and claimed transformation correspondence. Image and instruction-provenance claims additionally require the existing derivation checks, instruction mappings and multiplicities.\n\n   Preserve the distinction between compiled pre-stamp roots and transformed stamped roots. Reconstruct their explicit correspondence. Reuse recorded admission coordinates and expansion attempts; replay must not mint replacement origins.\n\n   The profile must pin the actual post-port stamping implementation. The [cc3 rebind note, item 3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794519755000-engineer-post-port-oracle-reverification.glm-5.3.findings.md:45) identifies two inserted applications per wrapper, site extraction at `[:operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`, and translated argument steps `[3 1] [3 2]`. Those details require executable dependency retention, not merely a commit string or copied counts.\n\n5. **Refusal and authority.**  \n   Missing capture, unavailable dependencies, unsupported profiles or source forms, reader failure, fidelity mismatch, root divergence, source-hash mismatch, or correspondence/coverage mismatch refuse the affected reconstruction claim. Report the failing compilation and stage. A corpus-wide claim cannot silently omit failed fixtures.\n\n   Do not recover by sorting collections, switching to decoded-value compilation, weakening equality, changing expected roots, or manufacturing origins. Those actions would change the recipe or evidence.\n\n   Refusal does not erase historical evidence or prohibit ordinary loading under existing code contracts. A producer’s successful gate establishes eligibility for independent verification; only the separate §4.2 verification result authorizes reliance.\n\n6. **Versioning and retention.**  \n   Assign this text replay profile its own immutable identity and retain its interpreter/dependency closure. The existing closed D1 envelope and recipe schema can carry it through `profile`, `inputs` and `input-roles`; no Jing, UAST or manifest schema change is required solely for this amendment. The exact oracle evidence contract remains unchanged.\n\nThe migration is:\n\n1. Keep the current code and r3 results classified as a feasibility pilot. No accepted corpus record exists to migrate.\n2. Move capture to the input boundary and implement the versioned text profile, including replay-preserving `forms`/`call` inputs and explicit compiler arguments. Remove the redundant decoded `:program` from the authoritative input contract; retain it only as optional diagnostic evidence if useful.\n3. Implement admission and reopened-store refusal gates for every corpus compilation. Preserve the observed decoded-value divergence as a regression case; include metadata, collection-kind, map/set order and unreadable-input cases.\n4. Produce new durable admissions where the pilot’s retained closure no longer exists. Never reconstruct historical admission identity from equal source text. Preserve the frozen oracle evidence and establish each new compilation’s relationship to it independently.\n5. Complete dependency retention, compilation-scoped bindings, discovery/retention ownership and independent verification before publishing a relying companion. The r2/r3 limits on tagged execution and measurements remain in force.\n\nStatic review found these concrete pilot deficiencies:\n\n| Location | Finding and required correction |\n|---|---|\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:2545](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2545) | Text is printed after compilation at line 2523, and only three printer bindings are controlled. This establishes neither capture before consumption nor a complete printing profile. Move capture earlier and pin the remaining behavior. No mutation-induced corruption is demonstrated by this review. |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:2544](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2544) | Only `program` gets a text representation. `forms` and `call` remain decoded values, while [the source-hash function](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2429) uses traversal order for gensym assignment and then prints the result. Program-root replay alone leaves this second order-sensitive dependency unverified. Preserve these inputs appropriately without changing the frozen hash algorithm. |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:4274](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:4274) | Bare `edn/read-string` supplies no explicit profile and does not establish an exactly-one-form/EOF contract. The compiler’s `true` argument is also supplied by the test rather than identified in a retained recipe. |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:4278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:4278) | Host `=` and `content=` do not establish metadata or syntax-kind fidelity. [The latter’s implementation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:540) explicitly ignores metadata and equates sequential kinds. Add the profile’s syntax/metadata checks; preserve root equality as a separate obligation. |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:4285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:4285) | The replay compares a root, while retained metadata is merely fetched and compared with the original producer output. It does not reconstruct C1, source hash, declarations or stamped correspondence after reopen, and checks only the stored root row directly. This remains a pilot, not a complete reconstruction gate. |\n\nThe structural storage helpers already disclaim verification; their ability to store an unverified claim is not itself a defect. Their success must not become the acceptance signal.\n\nStatic review only. No files edited and no suites run."}}
